<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Two Towers (Bologna)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Asinelli Tower, Garisenda Tower</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Two Towers, the Asinelli and the Garisenda, are medieval towers in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the city's best-known landmarks. Built in the early 12th century by noble families, they are survivors of the many towers raised in the commune. The Asinelli is about 97 metres tall, while the shorter Garisenda leans markedly and is mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> in the Inferno.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/two-towers-bologna.jpg" alt="Two Towers (Bologna)" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vanni Lazzari · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asinelli_e_Garisenda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
